  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1251 PM MDT Wed Aug 26 2026\n\nSome weak moisture continues to spill over the ridge this \nafternoon. Afternoon showers and thunderstorms will form on most\nterrain features today. Storms are moving east-southeast thus \nfar and will continue this trajectory. While moisture sees a \nslight downturn this afternoon, there are still expectations for\na few isolated showers to pose flash flooding risks, should \nthey develop near susceptible washes, burn scars, and \nmountainsides. Showers will wane in the early evening hours and\nquiet conditions return overnight.\n\nHigh pressure doesn't budge much Thursday and persistence will\nrule with terrain based showers and thunderstorms. Model\nguidance suggests additional moisture spilling over the ridge\nThursday. Height falls precipitated by the inbound PACNW storm\ndrive some additional forcing. This looks like potential for a\nfew storms to extend their life cycle. Models hint at some\novernight showers continuing too. Training storms and/or longer\nlife cycles could produce some flash flooding again Thursday.\n\nPattern shift begins in earnest Friday with the ridge continuing\nto decay/push eastward. This will bring afternoon winds up in\nthe southwesterly flow Friday and Saturday, including some \nlocalized critical fire weather conditions. Additional shower \nand thunderstorm activity can be expected Friday afternoon, as \nanother pulse of moisture spills over the collapsing ridge. The \nnortheast Pacific remains an active upstream impact to the \npattern shift Saturday and beyond. A deeper pool of cooler, \nwetter air arrives Saturday. This, dare I say, looks more fall- \nlike given the erosion of the monsoonal high pressure pattern. \nI'm sure we still have some warm days ahead of us, but this next\nweek looks cooler and wetter. Tropical storm activity in the\neastern Pacific could play into local moisture supplies later\nnext week. This plays into September climatology and could\nsupply some needed moisture to the West Slope...fingers \ncrossed.",
